imes and continue  doing that until I ran into the first  card the pronunciation of the meaning  again then I wrote it out ten times and  then I put it across you know three or  two or three commas further over so it's  a very basic kind of call it spaced  repetition system nowadays there are  many more modern effective ways of  learning characters I got to the point I  started at learning ten a day I got up  to 30 a day with the understanding that  you're going to forget two-thirds of  what you learn so I had to keep on  reshuffling the learned cards back into  my deck because you have to learn and  relearn learn and relearn it's like so  much in language learning at first it  seems impossible I mean the character  for I walk has like I don't know how  many strokes in it you got to remember  I'm your yeah yeah I mean it seems  impossible but it's amazing what the  brain will eventually learn if you stay  with it and the big thing with learning  characters is it's true for all I'm  learning but especially for characters  do it every day every day just you know  that salami or Bologna salami slice  technique if you just keep doing it hey  you won't slip back as much you won't  forget as much and you'll eventually get  there and I use my technique for the  first thousand characters and thereafter  with enough reading and you know when I  encounter a new power character you know  writing it out a few times eventually  they start to stick because you start to  become more familiar with them you know  it's like everything in language during  the more things that you are now  familiar with the more things you notice  so you notice the components of the  characters when I first started them  they I was told that these are the  radicals these are the components that  represent you know water or metal or  something it was meaningless to me but  after a while you start to see those  things and so it becomes easier and  easier to learn the characters you just  have to  stay there course all right some people  say should you learn traditional or or  simplified it depends where you're going  to be but at the very least learn  simplified if you're going to be  involved with mainland China the vast  majority of Chinese people in the world  use the simplified system I started with  the traditional and then move to  simplified which is actually an easy way  to go because once you have the  traditional you can easily learn the  simplified it's kind of handy to have  both if you're in Taiwan or Hong Kong  you can pick up a newspaper read it and  understand it the language itself once  you get past okay the tones here again  when you learn the language every word  that you learn in the dictionary or in  the pinyin will have a pone mark so the  four tones are for use ma ma ma ma ma so  you have sort of a high flat tone ma ma  which is actually starts a little lords  ma ma ma now depending how these words  show up together some of those tones  actually modify themselves a little bit  and so ultimately you end up learning  the tones as part of little phrases or  little because again one other thing in  Chinese is a word as we understand a  word like understand or say improve it's  a good example improve or change  okay so guide yeah guys change en is  also changed guide then guy Shan is to  improve sugai is to revise so most of  the words in Chinese are actually  composed of two or three characters and  if you know one or two of the characters  and you see how they're now connected  with some other character you can kind  of intuitively figure out the meaning or  at least the words are easier to  remember so the more characters you have  for every character you have you  actually potentially have three or four  or five words because these now can be  combined like building blocks with other  characters to form two character or  three character  or even phrases so I digressed a bit  from the tone so after a while you  realize that you kind of have to learn  the tones not so much as individual you  know characteristics of each single  character but rather as part of phrases  because that way you get into the  intonation and because it's very  difficult while speaking every time you  go to use a word that trying to  remembers its first on second talk it's  very difficult to do but if you're used  to certain phrases and a certain  intonation of these phrases you'll  eventually start improving and you don't  want to be so tongue-tied and hung up  about the tones that you can't speak so  I for example found that my tones  improved over time was a constant battle  I might have eight hitting them 40% at  first and it just drives me moved up and  made out now at 80% when I get excited  and I want to emphasize something that's  actually a third tone or like yo Mayo  like do you have your I might hate you  because I'm keen to say something and  then my inclination in English to put  the tone where the emphasis is takes  over and I no longer use the tone as in  Chinese to indicate meaning remember  that we have tones in English there's  nothing unique or difficult or strange  about having tones in English we use  tones for emphasis in the sentence but  in Chinese they use tones ma ma ma ma to  differentiate meaning and so at times we  have a tendency when we're speaking to  want to use intonation for emphasis  rather than for the meaning of the words  but you have to believe that you'll  eventually get better at it and not get  too hung up about it in terms of the  structure the structure of Chinese it's  actually very rational I think it's a  very you know in its simplicity it's a  very efficient language so you go  Nietzsche okay I go watch you you don't  go near budge in here Boo is actually  fourth tone but if it's boo too because  the choose a fourth on it becomes second  don't worry about it but it helps me  Poochie then are you going need to butch  it you go not go so you know fairly  straightforward stuff in as in every  language you should focus on learning  the W words why where when etc you know  like why is way shoma and then because  you know Asia the UN way too much you  know you learned some of these key  connector words learn I spent a lot of  time in Chinese learning key phrases you  know all the things having to do with  why because although you know  Suellen is although swear and some Shama  Shama is what so straight as yama what -  I still want to blah blah blah or even  though Joe Swan de da da da why I did so  there's a whole bunch of these patterns  the big thing for me in Chinese was  patterns and if I get a pattern I want  to find ten examples of those patterns  and then look for them in my listening  and reading all of the grammatical  explanations that I found in Chinese  grammar books I can't remember a single  word a single term that they invent to  describe Chinese grammar all I focused  on was patterns particularly as regards  the question words why when where et  cetera and the connector words you know  the although you know however and all  those kinds of words one little trick in  Chinese that the they don't really have  relative pronouns so that if instead of  saying the house which is on the hill  they'll say the on the hill house  and there's a little connector word the  and you gotta use to that but by and  large the grammar is pretty  straightforward vocabulary building once  you have characters is pretty  straightforward  ah this idea of sort of inscrutable  exotic strange you quickly get over that  I mean that's there with any language  initially perhaps more so of the Chinese  but once you get into it it becomes your  language and there's so much interesting  stuff when I study Chinese  we did a lot of reading of you know  German model and that stuff because the  Cultural Revolution was on him so we got  a real sense of the whole political Gang  of Four and stuff that was going on in  China but what was particularly  fascinating and I really recommend once  your Chinese improves is the literature  of the 20s and 30s because what happened  to China is you had this the the  traditional China actually was a foreign  dynasty the Ching dynasty which ruled  China and very traditional with all of  their traditional Confucian is stand  Buddhist and all of the traditional  culture somewhat isolationist if you  want all of a sudden bang run smack dab  into the Western world and in 1911 there  is what they call a sort of Shanghai go  means revolution and there is a Republic  and then there's the struggles with  warlords and all this kind of stuff it  is actually it's quite astounding to me  how quickly but we could call sort of  modern almost western-style  intellectuals thinkers political  thinkers novelists and there was this  outpouring of intellectual production in  China through the 20s 30s despite the  fact that there were you know warlords  fighting and the basically continuous  civil war between the Communists and the  Nationalists invasion from Japan they  had you know four colonies I mean it was  a mess  hunger famines and floods and yet there  was this outstanding intellectual  outpouring and that is some of the most  interesting Chinese to read to me I  would also wonder one day get back to my  Chinese in order to read more about  Chinese history because there are so  many stories from Chinese history and  Chinese people are very much aware of  stories that come out of Chinese history  from 2,000 years ago and they make  reference to this and of course I  haven't had the time to spend on Chinese  because I studied it 45 years ago and  since then I've lived in Japan and I've  learned other languages and really  haven't spent enough time with Chinese  but all of that world awaits you not to  mention the modern Chinese and this is  one other thing  I'll end up with and that is that when I  study Chinese mainland China was kind of  closed it was you know Mao is China  people were afraid to talk to you  whereas now you can go there you can  make friends you can go to restaurants  to bars you know it's modernizing  there's trains there's their problems  there's corruption pollution and lack of  democracy and all those other things but  it's it's alive and and the Chinese are  very approachable there they have their  pluses and minuses like every national  group they're endearing qualities and  they're less enduring qualities when  we're talking generalities and yet you  find individuals with all kinds of  different personalities but generally  speaking I say that they are quite  approachable they love to talk and if  you speak their language they're  extremely supportive so there you have  it a brief intro to Chinese and I wish  you all the best of luck in learning  Chinese bye for now
 

